 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+The cache uses a 2Q replacement policy to resist sequential scan pollution while retaining frequently revisited comparison sheets:
+- **FIFO Queue ($A1$ / In-queue)**: Newly admitted completed sheets enter this queue first, occupying up to a bounded portion of cache capacity (e.g. 25–50%).
+- **LRU Queue ($Am$ / Main queue)**: Sheets referenced again while present in the cache are promoted to the main LRU queue.
+- **Ghost Out-queue ($A1_{out}$)**: Tracks keys (identifiers only, without payload bytes) evicted from $A1$. If a requested sheet misses the payload cache but hits $A1_{out}$, its subsequent completed render bypasses $A1$ and is admitted directly to $Am$.
+- When admitting a new sheet requires space, eviction targets $A1$ first if it exceeds its quota, otherwise evicting from $Am$ via LRU.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache Eviction Policy | 2Q replacement policy | LRU, Frequency-based eviction with aging | Protects repeatedly referenced comparison sheets from eviction during long sequential screenings without unbounded counter maintenance or stale retention. |
